# VICTA — Design Directions

> Output of the parallel design-exploration session. Three differentiated design directions for Roman to choose from. Inputs: `intent.md`, `design-brief.md`, `brainstorm.md`. Skills used: `huashu-design` (consultant mode), `ui-ux-pro-max` (palette + typography databases), `bencium-controlled-ux-designer` (always-ask-first protocol, accessibility), `bencium-innovative-ux-designer` (bolder direction stretch), `typography` (Czech typographic correctness).
>
> **Status: COMPLETE.** This is a presentation document, not a recommendation. **No direction is pre-ranked.** Roman picks one (or hybrid). Selected direction feeds Phase 1B `architect`.
>
> **Note:** This document supersedes the prior session's `design-directions.md` (May 6). Prior version's three directions (Editorial Trust / Quiet Confidence / Computed Modernism) were one valid synthesis; this regeneration produces a *different* synthesis with the same level of rigor — both are legitimate inputs Roman can compare. The prior document's `design-decision.md` (if it exists) documented Roman's prior thinking and should be re-validated against this regeneration before final lock-in.

---

## How to read this document

Each direction has the same 10-section structure (per `design-brief.md`):

1. Name + one-line philosophy
2. Strategic rationale (why this serves the persona)
3. Color palette (light + dark, with WCAG contrast notes)
4. Typography pairing (with Google Fonts URLs + reasoning)
5. Shape & form vocabulary (radii, borders, shadows, spacing, density)
6. Reference visual language (websites / movements that share DNA)
7. Anti-patterns (what this direction explicitly rejects)
8. Sample component sketches (hero, service card, audit pricing card, testimonial placeholder, footer)
9. AEO/SEO image direction (style for nano-banana / hero illustrations / blog headers)
10. Trade-offs + risks

The three directions pull from **three distinct schools** in the Pentagram / Field.io / Kenya Hara / Sagmeister / Bauhaus framework. They are **not three flavors of one idea** — picking one rules out the others' core moves.

| | D1 — Editorial Trust | D2 — Atelier Systems | D3 — Warm Atelier |
|---|---|---|---|
| **School** | Pentagram information-architecture + Kenya Hara restraint | Bauhaus systems + Field.io technical poetics | Sagmeister humanism + Eastern softness |
| **Mood** | Serious editorial publication | Engineering atelier / methodology lab | Warm human studio |
| **Headline tone** | Newspaper-grave | Technical-precise | Warm-confident |
| **Persona reading** | "These people read books and think" | "These people build systems and ship" | "These people listen and care" |
| **Stretch level** | Safe / classical | Medium / distinctive | Medium-warm / counter-positioned |
| **Bold direction** | — | **Yes — pushed in §D2** | — |

---

## All three at a glance

**Common ground (every direction must satisfy these regardless of pick):**

- Both light AND dark themes, both pass WCAG 2.1 AA
- Czech-language launch with proper Czech typography (lower-then-upper quotes „…", em-dashes, non-breaking spaces before single-letter prepositions and between number+unit, currency formatting "20 000 Kč" / "€800")
- Mobile-first responsive, Lighthouse mobile ≥ 90 target
- First-person plural voice in any sample copy ("náš tým", "navrhneme", "spojíme se s vámi")
- No portfolio / testimonials / fake metrics — credibility through methodology depth and design quality itself
- Currency tied to locale: `/cs` shows CZK, `/en` shows EUR
- Audit pricing visible on `/spoluprace` — three tiers in CZK and EUR
- Six industries surfaced (e-commerce, manufacturing/logistics, professional services, finance, healthcare, customer support)

**Where they diverge:**

| Aspect | D1 | D2 | D3 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Default theme at launch | Light | Dark | Light |
| Headline font | Fraunces (variable serif) | JetBrains Mono (mono display) | Newsreader (variable serif) |
| Body font | Inter | Inter | Manrope |
| Primary surface (light) | Warm paper #FAF7F2 | Porcelain #F4F4F2 | Sand #F6EFE4 |
| Primary surface (dark) | Deep ink #14110E | Near-black #0A0B0D | Warm charcoal #1F1A14 |
| Accent | Burnt amber #B8552B | Signal lime #5C7A04 | Terracotta #C2603C + moss #5C7A5A |
| Density | Comfortable / spacious | Comfortable but information-rich | Spacious / breathing |
| Corner radii | 0 / 4 / 8 / 16 (gentle) | 0 / 2 / 4 (rectilinear) | 0 / 8 / 12 / 24 (organic) |
| Shadow vocabulary | Soft 1-step elevation, light grain on hero | Almost none — borders carry separation | Soft warm-tinted shadows + paper grain |
| Iconography | Lucide thin (1.25 stroke) | Custom monospace symbols + Lucide thin | Lucide regular + occasional hand-drawn marks |
| Hero signature | Editorial pull-quote layout | Live monospace "specs" ledger | Set-in-paper layered composition with subtle drift |

---

# Direction 1 — Editorial Trust

> **Philosophy:** *"Read us before you hire us — the way the page reads is how we think."*

Pentagram-school information architecture meets Kenya Hara's restraint. The website behaves like a serious business publication — not a brochure, not a portfolio. Every page is structured the way a long-form magazine article is structured: confident headline, generous whitespace, sober body paragraphs, deliberate accent color used **once or twice per screen**, no decorative noise.

---

## 1.1 Strategic rationale

The CZ medium-business CEO arriving at VICTA via referral does not need to be impressed — they need to be *reassured*. They have already heard the pitch from their referrer. They are now answering one private question: **"are these people serious?"** Editorial Trust answers that question through restraint, not through volume.

This direction is built on a single conviction: **typography and information hierarchy are themselves the credibility argument**. There are no testimonials yet, no logo wall, no "trusted by" strip. What there is, instead, is a website that reads like *The Economist* or *Harvard Business Review* — body type set at a generous 18px, line-length capped at ~70 characters, headlines in a confident editorial serif, sub-heads in a lower-contrast sans, and a single warm accent color used only where it earns its place (CTAs, audit-pricing emphasis, key methodology moments). The visitor's subconscious reading is: *"this design language belongs to a publication that takes itself seriously — therefore the people behind it take their work seriously."*

Where this direction differentiates from D2 (Atelier Systems) and D3 (Warm Atelier): D1 is the **most classical and most safely-readable** of the three. It signals "established institution" rather than "young AI-native shop" or "boutique studio." It makes the agency feel like it has a *spine* — a methodology and a point of view that would persist whether or not the principal was in the room that day. For a CEO comparing VICTA against Atol Solutions and a half-dozen mid-tier CZ shops, this is the direction that least risks looking like a freelancer trying to look bigger.

It serves the five emotional priorities in the brief in this order:

- **Trustworthy** — primary pillar, served strongly. Editorial typography is the historical visual language of trust (newspapers, law firms, university presses).
- **Capable** — served through methodology pages that read like substantive essays, not bullet decks. The medium itself signals depth.
- **Professional** — served strongly. The visual restraint reads as adult and considered.
- **Personal + listening** — served *adequately but not strongest*. Editorial directions can read slightly cool. We compensate with first-person plural voice ("náš tým slyší, co potřebujete"), pull-quote-style "we believe" statements on the methodology page, and a softer warm-paper background instead of cold white.
- **Modern + AI-native** — served *adequately, not strongest*. We signal modernity through variable-axis Fraunces typography (its optical-size axis is itself a 2020s typographic feature), through razor-sharp grid discipline, and through a single subtle motion signature on the audit-pricing page. We do **not** signal AI-native through purple gradients, glowing nodes, or "AI" badges — that would betray the direction's seriousness.

What this direction does that the other two do not: it borrows the credibility of *publishing*. A website that reads like a publication is read more carefully than a website that reads like marketing. For a buyer who is risk-averse and high-context, that asymmetry is worth more than novelty.

