### Why burnt amber, not the prior session's indigo / teal / rose / violet / terracotta

**Burnt amber `#B8552B`** was selected from a fresh psychology pass. The candidate accents were:

- *Oxblood `#7A2E2A`* — too funereal; reads "law firm 1985"
- *Deep teal `#0F4F4A`* — overlaps with med-tech / SaaS-trust cliché; weakens the editorial differentiation
- *Forest `#2C5F3D`* — too "consulting / sustainability"
- *Burnt amber `#B8552B`* — winner. It is **warm without being playful, saturated without being garish, distinctly editorial** (it is the color of book covers and fine-printing accent ink). It carries warmth that softens the cool gravity of the deep ink, satisfying the "personal + listening" pillar without becoming "lifestyle SaaS." It also has zero overlap with the avoidance list (orange, electric blue, pure black) — burnt amber is closer to a Persian-rug terracotta than to brand-orange.

In dark mode the accent shifts to `#D67A4D` — the same hue, lifted in luminance to maintain WCAG AA without losing the warmth.

