## 1.3 Typography pairing

| Role | Font | Weights used | Google Fonts |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Display / headlines** | **Fraunces** (variable serif, Soft + opsz axes) | 400, 500, 600, 700 | https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Fraunces |
| **Body** | **Inter** (variable) | 400, 500, 600 | https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Inter |
| **UI / labels** | Inter (smaller sizes, +letter-spacing) | 500, 600 | (same import) |
| **Mono** | **JetBrains Mono** | 400, 500 | https://fonts.google.com/specimen/JetBrains+Mono |

**Combined Google Fonts URL:**

```
https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:opsz,wght@9..144,400;9..144,500;9..144,600;9..144,700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap
```

### Reasoning for the pairing

**Fraunces** is what makes this direction work. It is a 2020s variable serif with an `opsz` (optical-size) axis and a `SOFT` axis — meaning the same family scales from a hard, text-grade form at 14–18px to a softer, more humanist display form at 64+. That single axis covers what would otherwise need three separate type families. The `SOFT` axis is also where the "personal + listening" emotional pillar is paid for: at low SOFT it reads like a serious modernist serif (think *Wallpaper\* magazine*); at high SOFT it reads warmer, almost humanist. We tune SOFT 30–40 for headlines (warm-but-confident) and SOFT 0 for any tiny serif use.

**Inter** carries body and UI. It is the global modern neo-grotesque standard (used by Linear, Vercel, GitHub) — it disappears, which is what a body face for a credibility-validator site is supposed to do. It also has *exemplary* Czech diacritic coverage (every diacritic in Latin Extended-A correctly drawn) and supports tabular numerics for the audit pricing tables.

**JetBrains Mono** appears only in three places: code blocks, the contact-form input field for a developer-friendly feel (mono placeholder text in the chatbot input), and as labels for the audit-tier reference IDs ("Tier 1 / 2 / 3"). It is restrained — never used for hero copy. (Hero copy uses Fraunces. Always.)

**The pairing's psychology**: serif headline + neo-grotesque body is the **most-trusted typographic pairing in 20th-century print** — it is the structure of *The New York Times*, *The Economist*, *Le Monde*, *The Guardian*. By inheriting that structure we inherit its credibility. Adding JetBrains Mono in tightly scoped places signals modern engineering competence without surrendering the editorial center of gravity.

### Czech typography (validated against `typography` skill rules)

All three fonts ship full Czech diacritic coverage in Google Fonts' Latin Extended subset (verified for: `á č ď é ě í ň ó ř š ť ú ů ý ž`, both lower and upper case). Czech-specific rules applied to all Direction 1 sample copy:

- Quote marks: `„…"` (lower-then-upper, German-style, *not* English curly). HTML entities: `&bdquo;` and `&ldquo;`. Apostrophes: `&rsquo;`.
- Em-dash: `—` (U+2014, `&mdash;`), spaced with `&thinsp;` if crushed.
- En-dash: `–` (U+2013) for ranges (e.g., `20 000 – 90 000 Kč`).
- Single-letter prepositions never orphaned: `a&nbsp;`, `i&nbsp;`, `k&nbsp;`, `o&nbsp;`, `s&nbsp;`, `u&nbsp;`, `v&nbsp;`, `z&nbsp;` — applied via build-time CSS or post-processing in the content pipeline.
- Number + unit joined: `20&nbsp;000&nbsp;Kč`, `€800` (CZK after with nbsp; EUR before with nbsp).
- One space after every punctuation. No double spaces.
- Kerning + ligatures on by default: `font-feature-settings: "kern" 1, "liga" 1; text-rendering: optimizeLegibility;`.

### Type scale (1.25 ratio — major third)

| Token | px (desktop) | px (mobile) | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `text-xs` | 12 | 12 | Footnotes, legal |
| `text-sm` | 14 | 14 | Captions, metadata |
| `text-base` | 18 | 16 | Body copy |
| `text-lg` | 22 | 20 | Lead paragraphs, callouts |
| `text-xl` | 28 | 24 | Sub-heads (h3) |
| `text-2xl` | 36 | 30 | Section heads (h2) |
| `text-3xl` | 48 | 36 | Page heads (h1) |
| `text-display` | 72 | 48 | Hero only |

Body text 18px on desktop / 16px on mobile (mobile threshold preserved to avoid iOS auto-zoom). Line-height 1.55 for body, 1.15 for headlines, 1.30 for sub-heads.

---

## 1.4 Shape & form vocabulary

| Property | Value | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| **Corner radii** | `0 / 4 / 8 / 16` | Gentle but disciplined. Hero blocks `0` (printed-page edges). Cards `8`. Modals `16`. Pills (status badges) `9999` only for pricing tier badges. |
| **Border weights** | `0.5px / 1px / 2px` | 0.5 for subtle dividers, 1px standard, 2px for the "newspaper rule" under section titles. |
| **Shadow language** | Single-step elevation: `0 1px 2px rgba(20, 17, 14, 0.06), 0 4px 12px rgba(20, 17, 14, 0.04)`. Used **only** on modals, dropdowns, popovers — *never* on resting-state cards. Cards earn separation through borders + surface contrast, not glow. |
| **Spacing scale** | `4 / 8 / 12 / 16 / 24 / 32 / 48 / 64 / 96` (px) | 4-base, 8-rhythm. Hero vertical spacing 96. Section gap 64. Card padding 32. |
| **Density** | **Comfortable / spacious.** Cards have generous internal padding (32px). Hero gets 96px vertical padding. Service rows have 24px breathing room between rule and content. |
| **Grid** | 12-column desktop, 4-column mobile, 24px gutters. Max content width 1200px for marketing pages, 720px for long-form text (methodology, blog, audit detail copy). |
| **Iconography** | **Lucide thin (1.25 stroke)** at 18px / 20px / 24px. Used sparingly — never one-icon-per-bullet. |
| **Imagery** | Full-bleed editorial photography in hero only (or no image at all + large typography). nano-banana / Kling output should look like *FT Weekend* photo features, not stock. See §1.9. |
| **Motion** | Restrained: 200–250ms cubic-bezier easing on hover transitions; one signature page-load reveal where headline appears with a subtle stagger; no parallax, no scroll-jacking, no decorative motion. |

---

## 1.5 Reference visual language

1. **The Economist (online edition, 2023+ redesign)** — the editorial gravity, the use of one accent red sparingly, the body-text generosity. We borrow the *sense* of typographic seriousness, not the literal palette.
2. **Pentagram's own studio site (pentagram.com)** — particularly project pages: large generous heads, body set in a confident sans, restrained color, methodology presented as essay not deck.
3. **Werkplaats Typografie / Wim Crouwel-school documentation** — the discipline of grid-as-credential. We take the rigor without copying the Dutch-modern look.
4. **Buildinamsterdam.com** — already on Roman's reference list. We borrow the cinematic editorial heroes; we leave behind the agency-portfolio framing (since we have no portfolio).
5. **Are.na as a publication (are.na/blog)** — mature small-team publication aesthetic; warm-paper background, single accent, editorial pace.

---

## 1.6 Anti-patterns (this direction explicitly rejects)

1. **Glowing gradient hero backgrounds.** Pentagram editorial does not glow. The hero is paper-on-ink, not a screensaver.
2. **One icon per bullet.** Methodology lists are typographic, not visual. A bullet earns its place through prose, not through a Lucide check-circle.
3. **Cards-floating-on-gradient-mesh.** Cards live on the warm paper or on a sunken surface. No glassmorphism, no soft shadow halos.
4. **Animated "stats counters"** ("we have 47 happy clients" counting up). We have no stats. We are not pretending.
5. **Hero video loops of generic agency b-roll** (laptop screen, plant, designer-typing-hands stock). Hero is still — typography or one curated still image.
6. **Pure white `#FFFFFF` page background.** Warm paper `#FAF7F2`. White on light themes is the classic AI-generated tell.
7. **Sans-serif headlines.** This direction rules out a sans-headline alternative — that's what D2 is for.
8. **All-caps-letterspaced eyebrows everywhere.** Used at most once per page (for the section overline above a hero). Otherwise lowercase or sentence-case.

