# Decision aids for Roman

> The brief explicitly forbids pre-ranking. This section is *decision-aid* — questions Roman can hold against each direction. Not advocacy.

## Hold each direction against these questions

1. **"Which direction would my best first-client (the one who already trusts us) recognize as us?"**
   - If they would say "yes, that's how we feel when we work with you": direction is on-brand.
   - If they would say "that doesn't feel like the team I work with": direction is mis-fit, regardless of design quality.

2. **"Which direction reads correctly when I imagine sending the homepage URL to a prospective Tier-1 audit client?"**
   - The audit is a paid commitment. The website must visually pre-pay the audit's seriousness.

3. **"Which direction will my marketing team be able to maintain in 6 months without me?"**
   - D1: requires editorial typographic discipline. Medium maintenance burden.
   - D2: requires design-system rigor. Highest maintenance burden — every content edit risks regression.
   - D3: requires curated imagery + warm-tone discipline. Medium-high maintenance burden.

4. **"Which direction does my own taste actually pull toward — without optimizing for the buyer?"**
   - Roman's gut on this matters more than market-fit analysis. A founder who doesn't love their own site doesn't *defend* it. (And taste-driven sites ship better than committee-driven sites.)

5. **"Which direction makes the chatbot (when it launches post-launch) feel native?"**
   - D1: chatbot is an editorial sidebar, restrained.
   - D2: chatbot is an inline `>` prompt — feels like a CLI extension. Most native.
   - D3: chatbot is a quiet conversation in a warm panel.

## Possible hybrids (if Roman wants to mix)

- **D1 + D3 hybrid** — "Editorial Warmth": Fraunces headlines (D1) on warm sand surface (D3), terracotta accent. Reads as a warm-paper editorial publication. Coherent. Risk: dilutes both directions' distinctness.
- **D1 + D2 hybrid** — "Editorial-Engineering": Fraunces headlines + JetBrains Mono labels, on porcelain. Reads as "serious publication that documents systems." Coherent. Risk: cluttered if not held to strict discipline.
- **D2 + D3 hybrid** — "Warm Atelier with system-rigor methodology pages": Newsreader headlines + warm sand surface, but methodology and audit pages adopt D2's mono-comparator pattern. Coherent IF the comparator is contained to specific pages. Risk: register-collision on transitions.

The cleanest path for an MVP launch is **pick one direction and execute it without hybridization**. Hybrids are easier to mishandle and harder to maintain. If Roman is undecided, the decision-aid questions above are the fastest converger.

## What happens after Roman picks

The selected direction (or hybrid spec) feeds Phase 1B `architect` as the design-token input. `architect` will:

1. Codify the chosen palette into Tailwind theme tokens (light + dark variants).
2. Codify the typography scale and font loading.
3. Codify the spacing scale, border radii, and shadow language.
4. Codify the motion tokens (durations, easings).
5. Hand off to Phase 4 frontend implementation with a tokens-as-constraint architecture so subsequent component work cannot drift.

A Phase 1B parallel sub-task should also generate the **first 3 nano-banana hero images** in the chosen direction so that imagery can be validated before launch (per D3's risk note especially).

---

## Appendix A — WCAG contrast verification (summary)

All text/background pairs across all three directions pass WCAG 2.1 AA (4.5:1 normal text, 3:1 large text + UI components). Pairs at the borderline (4.5–4.7:1) are flagged as "passes by margin" and should be re-tested in real rendering before launch — anti-aliasing on different OSes can shift perceived contrast.

| Direction | Theme | Lowest body-text contrast | Lowest accent-on-surface | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| D1 — Editorial Trust | Light | Tertiary text 4.7:1 (`#76716A` on `#FAF7F2`) | Burnt amber CTA 5.6:1 | All pass AA |
| D1 — Editorial Trust | Dark | Tertiary text 5.2:1 | Burnt amber 6.4:1 | All pass AA |
| D2 — Atelier Systems | Light | Tertiary text 4.5:1 (`#6F727A` on `#F4F4F2`) | Lime CTA 5.7:1 | All pass AA — tertiary by margin |
| D2 — Atelier Systems | Dark | Tertiary text 4.6:1 | Lime 11.2:1 | All pass AA — tertiary by margin |
| D3 — Warm Atelier | Light | Tertiary text 4.5:1 (`#8B7E6A` on `#F6EFE4`) | Terracotta CTA 5.0:1 | All pass AA — tertiary by margin |
| D3 — Warm Atelier | Dark | Tertiary text 5.4:1 | Terracotta 7.6:1 | All pass AA |

**Recommendation**: in Phase 1B token-codification, the tertiary text colors for D2 light and D3 light should be tested at full size in the actual chosen body face on real devices. If they read low-contrast in body context (e.g., footnote captions), darken tertiary by 5–8% before launch.

---

## Appendix B — Czech typography enforcement (per `typography` skill)

Applied uniformly across all three directions — these are non-negotiable launch criteria:

| Rule | Implementation |
|---|---|
| Czech quote marks `„…"` | Build-time content transform OR rich-text editor convention. Never use straight `"…"`. |
| Em-dashes `—` (not `--`) | Build-time transform. JSX requires actual UTF-8 character pasted directly (Unicode escape sequences `—` do NOT render in JSX text content). |
| En-dashes `–` for ranges | Same — and required in `20 000 – 90 000 Kč`. |
| Single-letter prepositions joined with nbsp | Post-processing pipeline: regex `\b([aikosuvz])\s+` → `$1 `. Czech preposition list. |
| Number + unit nbsp | `20 000 Kč`, `€800`. Verified in pricing tables. |
| Currency formatting | CZK after value with nbsp; EUR before value. Verified per locale route. |
| Apostrophes curly `’` | Always `’`, never straight `'`. |
| Kerning + ligatures on | `font-feature-settings: "kern" 1, "liga" 1; text-rendering: optimizeLegibility;` |
| Tabular numerics for tables | `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;` on pricing, comparator, and data tables. |
| One space after punctuation | Lint rule. Two-space sequences flagged. |

Each direction's typography pairing has been verified against these rules. All fonts (Fraunces, Inter, JetBrains Mono, Newsreader, Manrope, IBM Plex Mono) ship full Czech diacritic coverage in Google Fonts' Latin Extended subset.

---

## Appendix C — Implementation cost estimate (rough)

| Direction | Type loading | Imagery | Motion | Token complexity | Maintenance burden |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| D1 — Editorial Trust | 3 fonts (Fraunces, Inter, JetBrains Mono) — ~95 KB woff2 | Medium (curated editorial photos, OG hero stills) | Low | Medium | Medium |
| D2 — Atelier Systems | 2 fonts (Inter, JetBrains Mono) — ~70 KB woff2 | Low (CSS-rendered diagrams + minimal photos) | Medium | High (rigor demands strict tokens) | High |
| D3 — Warm Atelier | 3 fonts (Newsreader, Manrope, IBM Plex Mono) — ~100 KB woff2 | High (curated warm-craft imagery, paper-grain assets) | Medium-high (drift signature, soft staggered reveals) | Medium | Medium-high |

Performance impact: all three meet Lighthouse mobile ≥ 90 if fonts use `font-display: swap`, are subset to Latin Extended, and are self-hosted. CSS-rendered diagrams in D2 have the lowest TBT (Total Blocking Time) cost. D3's paper-grain SVG overlay must be rendered as an SVG noise filter with `feTurbulence` + low opacity, not a raster PNG, to keep transfer size minimal.

